# Null Safety

## Core Rules

- Default to non-nullable types.
- Use `T?` only when absence is a real part of the domain contract.
- Prefer explicit handling with `if`, `??`, `?.`, and guards over force unwrap.
- Avoid `late` unless lifecycle or lazy initialization genuinely requires it.

## Modeling

- Do not use nullable fields to hide distinct states.
- Prefer sealed classes, enums, or dedicated value objects when states are
  better represented as explicit variants.
- Do not mark fields nullable just to postpone proper modeling decisions.

## API Design

- Use required named parameters for mandatory values.
- Return nullable values only when callers can safely model absence.
- Make public nullability contracts obvious in naming, types, and docs.

## Boundary Normalization

- Treat JSON, FFI payloads, native buffers, env vars, and external inputs as
  untrusted.
- Normalize absent or malformed values at the boundary before they spread
  through the package.
- Keep transport or native null semantics out of stable domain entities when a
  stronger internal model is available.

## Safe Patterns

```dart
final String displayName = user.nickname ?? 'Unknown';
final int count = items?.length ?? 0;

if (result case final value?) {
  use(value);
}
```

## Avoid

```dart
final int size = maybeList!.length;
```

Use force unwrap only when safety has already been proven in the same scope.
